# Shipping changes

Pushing to `main` triggers `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`, which builds and
publishes the extension image. The user then installs the new version manually
from the BlueOS extension manager.

So the delivery path for any change is: commit, push, and say the build is
running. Never present a hand-deploy as having shipped a change.

# Hot-patching the vehicle

Copying files into the running container is a legitimate way to *verify* a fix
against real hardware, and is often the only way to test something that needs
the camera or autopilot:

```bash
C=extension-vshieblueossubreelstowfishmain
docker cp main.py $C:/app/main.py && docker restart $C
```

It is not durable. The extension manager can recreate the container at any
time, silently reverting every copied file to whatever is in the local image
cache -- which may itself be older than the latest CI build. Both failures are
quiet, because the app keeps serving either way.

After hot-patching, confirm the new code is actually running instead of
assuming the copy took effect:

```bash
docker exec $C grep -c some_symbol_from_the_change /app/main.py
docker inspect $C --format 'created={{.Created}}'   # recreated behind you?
```

Anything verified this way still has to be committed and pushed to ship.
